AMOS: Adaptive Memory Orchestration System for Physical Memory Objects

A Constrained Sequential Decision Framework for Intelligent Memory Management in AI Systems

Version 3.2 — Final Theoretical Draft

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

AI CONTRIBUTION: chatGpt, Grok XAi, Meta AI, Deepseek 

Abstract

Modern AI systems are limited by memory capacity and bandwidth rather than arithmetic throughput. Existing allocators satisfy local requests efficiently but do not globally optimise memory usage under dynamic, multi‑tier, and resource‑constrained workloads. The Adaptive Memory Orchestration System for Physical Memory Objects (AMOS) reformulates memory management as a constrained sequential decision problem. It unifies retention, migration across hardware tiers, compression, recomputation, and eviction into a single decision space. AMOS rests on a Memory Value Function derived from a Lagrangian relaxation of a constrained Markov decision process, providing an economically interpretable index that guides per‑object actions. The framework couples a primal‑dual policy that adjusts shadow prices for shared resources with a safety shield that enforces instantaneous hard capacity constraints. This final theoretical draft resolves unit inconsistencies in the value function, formalises the separability assumptions needed for decomposition, sharpens the complexity model, and lists concrete open problems. The result is a rigorous, implementation‑aware foundation for the algorithmic development of provably efficient memory orchestration in next‑generation AI infrastructures.

---

1. Introduction

The memory wall has become the dominant performance bottleneck in large‑scale artificial intelligence. Large language models, diffusion networks, and multimodal agents generate working sets—parameters, activations, gradients, and key–value caches—that far exceed the capacity of on‑accelerator high‑bandwidth memory. The current response is a collection of point solutions: tiered offloading to CPU DRAM or CXL pools, activation checkpointing, tensor compression, and opportunistic recomputation. Each technique is governed by static, heuristic policies that treat memory objects in isolation and ignore system‑wide trade‑offs among latency, energy, and bandwidth.

This paper advocates a different perspective: memory management should be formulated as a global constrained sequential optimisation problem. The Adaptive Memory Orchestration System for Physical Memory Objects (AMOS) acts as a runtime orchestration layer above conventional allocators. For every physical memory object—a tensor, a KV‑cache entry, an intermediate feature map—it decides whether to Keep it in fast memory, Move it to a slower tier, Compress it, Recompute it on demand, or Evict it permanently. These decisions are made by a policy that minimises a long‑run cost of latency, energy, memory footprint, and bandwidth while respecting hard constraints on per‑tier capacity, power, and latency.

Disambiguation. The acronym AMOS is shared with several open‑source Agent Memory Operating System projects and with Atomic Memory Operations (AMOs) in NVSHMEM. This work exclusively addresses physical memory objects (tensors, KV‑caches) in hardware memory tiers; it has no relation to semantic agent memory.

New in the final theoretical draft (v3.2). Building on earlier conceptual sketches, this version (i) unifies the Memory Value Function around a unit‑consistent reduced form derived from a Lagrangian decomposition; (ii) provides a rigorous proposition with explicit separability assumptions that establish the index‑policy foundation; (iii) formalises the safety shield as an orthogonal projection onto the feasible action set; (iv) corrects the computational overhead estimate to include memory access and sorting costs; (v) sharpens the disambiguation language; and (vi) extends the list of open theoretical questions, including the sample complexity of reuse‑probability estimation under non‑stationary workloads.

