2. Related Work

Classical allocators and virtual memory. General‑purpose allocators (jemalloc, tcmalloc, mimalloc) and OS virtual memory subsystems (demand paging, working‑set theory) are designed for local efficiency—minimising fragmentation and allocation latency. Working‑set theory (Denning, 1966) formalises the concept of reuse distance but does not extend to the multi‑action decision space involving compression and recomputation.

Multi‑tier and CXL‑based memory for AI. Recent inference systems offload KV‑caches to CPU DRAM or NVMe, or employ CXL‑based shared memory pools such as Beluga (arXiv:2511.20172), which provides direct GPU load/store access to a large CXL‑switched pool. Rate‑distortion views of KV‑cache compaction treat compression as a bit‑allocation problem and eviction as the zero‑rate boundary. These methods do not unify migration, compression, and recomputation under a global resource‑constrained policy.

Agentic memory as sequential decisions. MemCon formulates agent memory operations as a Markov decision process with actions RETRIEVE and PLANINJECT, targeting semantic content. While it shares the decision‑theoretic spirit, it does not address physical tensors, multi‑tier hardware, bandwidth, or energy constraints. AMOS applies analogous ideas to a fundamentally different domain.

Constrained MDPs and safe RL. Constrained Markov decision processes (Beutler and Ross, 1985; Altman, 1999) augment an MDP with auxiliary cost functions and limits. Lagrangian and primal‑dual algorithms are standard tools for safe exploration. Lyapunov drift‑plus‑penalty methods (Neely) provide stability guarantees for stochastic network optimisation. AMOS adopts a hybrid of these techniques.

Gap. No existing framework unifies retention, migration, compression, recomputation, and eviction for physical memory objects as a single constrained sequential decision problem with an index policy grounded in a consistent economic valuation and explicit hard‑constraint enforcement.

