3. System Model and Problem Formulation

We model the memory system as a discrete‑time constrained Markov decision process (CMDP). Decision epochs occur on memory pressure events, allocation requests, or periodic re‑evaluation.

3.1 Memory Objects and State

Let  \mathcal{O}_t  be the set of live memory objects at time  t . Each object  i  is described by

x_{i,t} = (\,\text{size}_i,\; \text{tier}_i,\; \text{freq}_i(t),\; \hat{R}_i(t),\; C_i,\; e_i,\; b_i \;),

where  \hat{R}_i(t)  is the estimated reuse probability (reciprocal of expected reuse distance),  C_i  is the full recomputation cost,  e_i  is the energy rate of storing the object in its current tier, and  b_i  is the expected bandwidth demand ( \text{size}_i \times \text{freq}_i ). The global state is

s_t = \big( \{x_{i,t}\}_{i\in\mathcal{O}_t},\; \mathbf{M}_t,\; B_t,\; E_t,\; L_t,\; \mathbf{W}_t \big),

with  \mathbf{M}_t  the vector of available capacities per tier  k=1,\dots,K  (HBM, GPU DRAM, host DRAM, CXL, SSD),  B_t  aggregate bandwidth utilisation,  E_t  power draw,  L_t  latency penalty, and  \mathbf{W}_t  workload descriptors.

3.2 Action Space

For a single object the action set is

\mathcal{A} = \{ \text{Keep},\, \text{Move}(k),\, \text{Compress}(\rho),\, \text{Recompute},\, \text{Evict} \},

with parameters: Move(k) transfers to tier k, Compress(\rho) reduces size by factor \rho \in (0,1]. Recompute discards the object but retains reconstruction metadata, and Evict permanently removes it. A global action a_t is a vector of per‑object actions; only a candidate subset of objects is re‑evaluated at each epoch.

3.3 System Dynamics and Immediate Cost

The state evolves as  s_{t+1} = f(s_t, a_t, \omega_t) , where  \omega_t  captures exogenous workload variations. The one‑step cost is

c_t = \lambda_1 L_t + \lambda_2 E_t + \lambda_3 U(\mathbf{M}_t) + \lambda_4 B_t + \kappa \cdot \text{action\_overhead}(a_t),

with  U(\mathbf{M}_t)  a smooth barrier penalising high utilisation, and action_overhead the latency/energy penalty of migrations, compressions, etc.

3.4 Memory Value Function: Unit‑Consistent Definition

To guide decisions, we define the Memory Value Function (MVF) for object i in its reduced, unit‑consistent form:

\boxed{ \text{MVF}_i^{\text{(red)}} = \hat{R}_i(t) \cdot \big( C_i + \Delta E \big) - e_i },

where  \Delta E  is the energy cost of a cache miss (recomputation or slow‑tier access). All terms are expressed in the same unit (e.g., milliseconds of equivalent latency or Joules of energy). For bandwidth‑dominated objects, the term  C_i + \Delta E  can be extended by adding  \phi_B \cdot b_i , where  \phi_B  converts bandwidth consumption into the common unit. The object’s ranking index is then  \text{MVF}_i^{\text{(red)}} / \text{size}_i , i.e., value per unit capacity.

This definition avoids the unit inconsistency of a raw linear combination of heterogeneous quantities and directly links to the Lagrangian of a rate‑distortion problem: compression and eviction lie on a curve where the shadow price of capacity trades off distortion (recomputation/energy) against bit rate.

Proposition 1 (Decomposability and optimal threshold under capacity shadow price).
Assume:

· (A1) Additive costs: the system cost decomposes as  c_t = \sum_i c_{i,t} .
· (A2) Exogenous workload:  \omega_t  is independent of the actions taken.
· (A3) A single‑tier average capacity constraint is dualised with Lagrange multiplier  \lambda \ge 0 .

Then the Lagrangian  L(\pi,\lambda)  decouples across objects. For each object, the average‑cost Bellman equation reduces to a threshold rule: Keep the object if and only if

\text{MVF}_i^{\text{(red)}} > \lambda \cdot \text{size}_i + \text{eviction\_cost}.

This is precisely the structure of a Whittle‑style index policy. For the special case of a restless bandit with two actions (keep/evict), indexability holds and the MVF‑based ranking is optimal for the relaxed problem (Whittle, 1988). Indexability for the full five‑action set remains an open question.

Proof sketch. Under A1–A3 the Lagrangian is separable. The per‑object Bellman equation can be solved for the relative value of the Kept state. With stationary reuse statistics, the difference between the optimal cost of keeping and evicting yields the threshold. Formal details follow the standard restless‑bandit derivation and are omitted for brevity.

3.5 Optimisation Objective and Constraints

The policy  \pi  minimises the long‑run discounted or average cost  J(\pi) = \mathbb{E}\!\left[ \sum_t \gamma^t c_t \right] , subject to:

· Hard instantaneous constraints:  M_{k,t} \le M_{k,\max} ,  E_t \le E_{\max} ,  L_t \le L_{\max} .
· Time‑average constraints (optional), modelled as additional CMDP cost limits.

3.6 CMDP Formulation and Safety Shield

We formalise the problem as a CMDP with primary cost  c  and auxiliary costs  d_j  with limits  D_j . Because a pure CMDP cannot guarantee that instantaneous hard constraints hold at every step, we introduce a safety shield. Let

\mathcal{A}_{\text{feasible}}(s_t) = \{ a_t : M_{k,t+1} \le M_{k,\max} \text{ for all } k \}

be the set of actions that respect tier capacities in the next state. The shielded policy is the orthogonal projection of the CMDP policy onto this set:

\pi_{\text{shielded}}(s_t) = \text{Proj}_{\mathcal{A}_{\text{feasible}}(s_t)} \big( \pi_{\text{CMDP}}(s_t) \big).

If the projected action is empty (all tiers full), the shield forces immediate eviction of the objects with the lowest MVF‑per‑size. The shield guarantees hard constraint satisfaction, albeit possibly at the expense of global optimality.

3.7 Hardware Realism

Move and Compress actions are non‑instantaneous and may contend for shared interconnect bandwidth (e.g., PCIe, CXL). In‑flight migrations temporarily occupy both source and destination capacity and consume bandwidth. The safety shield therefore tracks in‑flight operations as pseudo‑objects that reserve capacity and bandwidth for their duration. The action overhead cost includes an estimate of transfer time, and the concurrency of migrations is capped to prevent interconnect saturation. These extensions fit within the same Lagrangian indexing framework, with the shadow price of bandwidth added as an additional multiplier.

