4. Structural Properties and Policy Design

4.1 Theoretical Building Blocks

Under assumptions A1–A3, the problem exhibits:

· Monotonicity: optimal keep/evict thresholds are monotone in reuse probability and memory pressure.
· Decomposability: dualising coupling constraints yields separable per‑object subproblems, leading to an index rule.
· Lyapunov stability: for time‑average constraints, a drift‑plus‑penalty rule (Neely) ensures stability.

4.2 Hierarchical Lagrangian Policy with MVF Indexing

Global level (slow timescale). Every  \tau  epochs, update Lagrange multipliers  \boldsymbol{\lambda} = (\lambda_{\text{tier}}, \lambda_E, \lambda_B)  using a gradient step based on measured constraint violations. When a tier’s occupancy approaches its limit,  \lambda_{\text{tier}}(k)  rises, making that tier “more expensive.”

Local level (per‑epoch).

1. Candidate set construction. Form  \mathcal{C}_t  as the union of the  N_1  least recently accessed objects, the  N_2  objects with the highest MVF^{\text{(red)}} per unit size, and a small random sample of size  N_3 . This hybrid avoids the blindness of pure LRU to high‑value cold objects and the insensitivity of pure MVF to very recently evicted items. Total candidate size  N = N_1 + N_2 + N_3  is bounded (e.g., 512).
2. Scoring. For each candidate  i  and each feasible action  a , compute
   \text{score}_i(a) = \text{MVF}_i^{(a)} - \lambda_{\text{tier}(a)} \cdot \text{mem\_usage}(a,i) - \text{action\_cost}(a),
   where MVF_i^{(a)} is the expected post‑action reduced MVF (e.g., after moving, the energy term changes) and \text{action\_cost}(a) includes transfer energy/latency.
3. Greedy allocation. Sort candidates by \text{score}_i(a^*) / \text{size}_i and assign actions in descending order, deducting tier capacities until full. Objects that cannot fit are forced to Evict (or Recompute if that offers higher long‑term value).

Safety shield. Before execution, project the resulting action vector onto  \mathcal{A}_{\text{feasible}}(s_t) . If any tier is over‑committed, downgrade the lowest‑scoring assignments until constraints are met. Limit concurrent migrations to the interconnect bandwidth budget.

Complexity. For  N = 512  candidates, 8 actions per object, and about 50 floating‑point operations per MVF evaluation, the arithmetic cost is negligible. Dominant costs are sorting the scores and the memory accesses needed to read object metadata. The per‑epoch wall‑clock overhead is < 50 µs including sorting and memory accesses, which is well below typical inter‑epoch intervals of 1–10 ms.

