Appendix B: Formal CMDP and Safety Shield

B.1 Constrained Markov Decision Process

A constrained Markov decision process (CMDP) is a tuple (\mathcal{S}, \mathcal{A}, P, c, \{d_j\}_{j=1}^m, \{D_j\}_{j=1}^m) where:

· \mathcal{S}: state space.
· \mathcal{A}: action space.
· P(s' | s, a): transition kernel.
· c: \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{A} \to \mathbb{R}: primary cost.
· d_j: \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{A} \to \mathbb{R}: auxiliary constraint costs.
· D_j: constraint limits on cumulative or time-average costs.

The objective is to find a stationary policy \pi minimizing the expected discounted (or average) primary cost J_c(\pi) = \mathbb{E}_\pi[\sum_{t} \gamma^t c_t] subject to J_{d_j}(\pi) \le D_j for all j.

In our setting, hard instantaneous constraints (e.g., M_{k,t} \le M_{k,\max}) cannot be directly expressed as expected cumulative constraints. We handle them via a projection (safety shield) while using CMDP for time-average limits.

B.2 Safety Shield as Projection

Define the feasible action set at state s_t:

\mathcal{A}_{\text{feasible}}(s_t) = \left\{ a_t \in \mathcal{A} \mid \forall k,\; M_{k,t+1} \le M_{k,\max} \right\},

where M_{k,t+1} is the tier-k memory usage resulting from executing action a_t in state s_t. The safety shield implements the policy:

\pi_{\text{shielded}}(s_t) = \argmin_{a \in \mathcal{A}_{\text{feasible}}(s_t)} \| a - \pi_{\text{CMDP}}(s_t) \|,

where \|\cdot\| is a suitable distance metric on action vectors. In practice, it greedily selects the highest-score actions that fit, and forces eviction of lowest-MVF objects when necessary. This projection guarantees that hard constraints are never violated, at the cost of potentially suboptimal long-run performance compared to an optimal unconstrained CMDP policy. The shield can be seen as a "safety layer" that restricts exploration and may slow learning but ensures safe operation.

