Appendix C: Lyapunov Drift and Stability

C.1 Drift-Plus-Penalty for Time-Average Constraints

For time-average constraints (e.g., average power \le E_{\max}), we can use the Lyapunov optimization framework. Define a virtual queue for constraint j with backlog Q_{j,t}, updated as:

Q_{j,t+1} = \max( Q_{j,t} + d_j(s_t, a_t) - D_j, 0 ).

The Lyapunov function L(\mathbf{Q}_t) = \frac{1}{2} \sum_j Q_{j,t}^2. The one-step conditional drift is \Delta(\mathbf{Q}_t) = \mathbb{E}[L(\mathbf{Q}_{t+1}) - L(\mathbf{Q}_t) \mid \mathbf{Q}_t]. Minimizing the drift-plus-penalty expression \Delta(\mathbf{Q}_t) + V \mathbb{E}[c_t \mid \mathbf{Q}_t] each step yields a policy that stabilizes the queues (ensuring time-average constraints are met) while achieving near-optimal primary cost, with optimality gap O(1/V) (Neely). In our hierarchical controller, the primal-dual updates for Lagrange multipliers approximate this.

