Appendix D: Complexity of the Per-Epoch Overhead

We provide a detailed breakdown of the computational cost per decision epoch.

Assumptions:

· Candidate set size N = 512.
· Number of actions per object |\mathcal{A}| = 8 (Keep, Move to up to 4 tiers, Compress with one ratio, Recompute, Evict).
· MVF evaluation cost: ~20 floating-point operations (additions, multiplications) plus accesses to object metadata (size, current tier, reuse probability, cost parameters) stored in a compact array. Assume metadata fits in L1 cache.
· Sorting of N objects by score/unit-size: O(N \log N) comparisons, each ~10 ns on a modern CPU.
· Memory access latency: assuming high cache hit rate, ~1 ns per access, total ~500 ns.

Estimated timing:

· Score computation: 512 \times 8 \times 20 = 81{,}920 FLOPs → negligible.
· Memory reads for metadata: ~512 objects × (5 fields) = 2560 reads, ~2.5 µs if all L1 hits.
· Sorting: 512 \log_2 512 \approx 4608 comparisons → ~46 µs.
· Total wall-clock time: ~50 µs, dominated by sorting. This is well below 1 ms inter-epoch intervals, confirming feasibility.

