Appendix E: Candidate Set Construction Details

The hybrid candidate set \mathcal{C}_t is built as:

\mathcal{C}_t = \mathcal{L}_t(N_1) \;\cup\; \mathcal{V}_t(N_2) \;\cup\; \mathcal{R}_t(N_3),

where:

· \mathcal{L}_t(N_1): the N_1 objects with the largest time since last access (LRU order).
· \mathcal{V}_t(N_2): the N_2 objects with the highest current \text{MVF}_i^{\text{(red)}} / \text{size}_i.
· \mathcal{R}_t(N_3): a uniform random sample of N_3 objects from the remaining live set, to provide exploration.

Typical values: N_1=384, N_2=64, N_3=64, yielding N=512. This construction ensures that both cold valuable objects and hot recently used objects are considered, while maintaining bounded complexity.

