Appendix F: Rate-Distortion Connection

The MVF threshold rule can be reinterpreted as the Lagrangian of a rate-distortion problem. Consider a memory object that can be stored in a compressed representation consuming r bits per unit of size (with uncompressed size normalized to 1). The distortion (quality loss) due to compression is a function D(r), where D(0) corresponds to full recomputation cost (eviction), and D(1) is zero (lossless keep). The total cost is D(r) + \lambda r, where \lambda is the shadow price of capacity. The optimal compression rate r^* solves \min_r D(r) + \lambda r. For many objects, the value of keeping vs. evicting is determined by the zero-rate boundary D(0) + \lambda \cdot 0 = \hat{R}_i (C_i+\Delta E), compared to the keep cost e_i + \lambda \cdot 1. The threshold rule emerges naturally. This connection to rate-distortion theory further justifies the MVF structure.

